  "Question": "If \\(y=x+\\dfrac1x,\\) then \\(x^4+x^3-4x^2+x+1=0\\) becomes:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x^2(y^2+y-2)=0\\)",
    "B": "\\(x^2(y^2+y-3)=0\\)",
    "C": "\\(x^2(y^2+y-4)=0\\)",
    "D": "\\(x^2(y^2+y-6)=0\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Dividing by \\(x^2\\) pairs the terms into expressions involving \\(x+1/x\\), which is exactly the given substitution. The substitution requires \\(x\\ne0\\); this excludes no original root because the original polynomial equals \\(1\\) at \\(x=0\\).\n\nSquaring the substitution gives\n\\[\ny^2=\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)^2\n=x^2+2+\\frac1{x^2},\n\\]\nso \\(x^2+1/x^2=y^2-2\\). We can therefore rewrite the original left side as\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^4+x^3-4x^2+x+1\n&=x^2\\left[\\left(x^2+\\frac1{x^2}\\right)\n+\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)-4\\right]\\\\\n&=x^2\\bigl[(y^2-2)+y-4\\bigr]\\\\\n&=x^2(y^2+y-6).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus the equation becomes \\(x^2(y^2+y-6)=0\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\), with \\(x\\ne0\\) still required.",
